---
metaTitle: Our process | How a coin is graded, step by step
metaDescription: 'From receipt to return: intake, authentication, attribution, grading, quality control, encapsulation, labeling, registry record and return shipping.'
eyebrow: Our process
heading: How a coin is graded here.
intro: From the moment a package arrives to the moment it leaves, every coin follows the same path. Each step has one purpose, and none is skipped.
sections:
  - mark: intake
    title: Receipt and intake
    summary: Each package is opened, checked against its submission and logged.
    text: |-
      When a package arrives, it is opened and its contents are checked against the submission it came with: the number of coins, their descriptions and the service requested.

      Each coin is then logged individually, so that it can be traced through every step that follows. If anything does not match the submission, we contact you before any work begins.
  - mark: authenticate
    title: Authentication
    summary: Is the coin genuine?
    text: |-
      Before anything else, the coin is examined to establish that it is genuine. Its weight, dimensions, style and fabric, and the marks of how it was made, are compared with what is known for the type.

      Where more evidence is useful, a specific gravity test can be carried out: it compares the coin's density with that expected of its metal, without harming the coin. A coin that cannot be authenticated is not encapsulated.
  - mark: attribute
    title: Attribution
    summary: What exactly is it?
    text: |-
      The coin is identified as precisely as it allows: issuer or authority, ruler, denomination, date or period, mint where it can be determined, and the variety or standard catalogue reference where one applies.

      For ancient coins at the services that include it, the identification is expanded and the coin's weight is recorded. A pedigree, the record of a coin's notable previous owners, can be noted on the label on request.
  - mark: grade
    title: Grading
    summary: How well has it been preserved?
    text: |-
      The coin is graded on its state of preservation: wear, strike, surfaces, luster and eye appeal, judged together rather than one at a time.

      Milled coins are graded on the 1 to 70 scale. Ancient coins, struck by hand, receive a descriptive grade with separate subgrades for strike and surface. Problems that affect a coin, such as cleaning or damage, are noted rather than hidden.
    points:
      - Read more in [Grading standards](/grading-standards).
  - mark: review
    title: Quality control
    summary: A second look before anything is sealed.
    text: Before a coin is sealed, its attribution, its grade and every detail destined for the label are reviewed again against the coin itself, so that what is printed is exactly what is in the holder.
  - mark: holder
    title: Encapsulation
    summary: Sealed for the long term.
    text: |-
      The coin is placed in a clear holder that is sonically welded around it, producing a permanent, tamper-evident seal. We use the same sealing techniques and machinery as other third-party grading services.

      The holder protects the coin from handling while leaving both sides in full view. Oversize holders are available for larger pieces.
  - mark: label
    title: Labeling and certification
    summary: The record, carried on the holder.
    text: |-
      Each holder carries a label with the coin's attribution, its grade and a unique certificate number.

      Every service includes a choice of our ready-made label designs for United States, world, Roman and Greek coins, each themed to its subject. Custom designs can be created for a collection.
  - mark: registry
    title: Registry record and verification
    summary: On record, and open to anyone.
    text: The certificate number and the details of the coin are entered in our certificate registry. Anyone holding the coin, now or decades from now, can type that number into this website and compare the record with the coin in hand.
    points:
      - '[Verify a certificate](/verify)'
  - mark: return
    title: Return shipping
    summary: Back to you, securely.
    text: Graded coins are packed for transit and returned to you by tracked mail. Signature delivery is required for orders valued over $750. We let you know when your coins are on their way.
coda:
  title: Questions about a particular coin?
  text: We are glad to discuss a coin or a group before anything is sent.
  links:
    - label: Grading standards
      href: /grading-standards
    - label: Contact
      href: /contact
---
